      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in triangle \\(VWX\\), we first need to determine the length of \\(WX\\). We know that \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\), \\(VW = 3\\), and \\(\\angle VWX\\) is a right angle (since \\(VWX\\) is a right triangle with \\(VW\\) and \\(WX\\) as the legs and \\(VX\\) as the hypotenuse).\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\(VWX\\), we have:\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\nSolving for \\(WX^2\\):\n\\[\nWX^2 = 13 - 9 = 4\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nWX = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\tan V\\). In a right triangle, \\(\\tan\\) of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. Here, the side opposite to \\(\\angle V\\) is \\(WX = 2\\) and the side adjacent to \\(\\angle V\\) is \\(VW = 3\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
