      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\tan V\\) in the right triangle \\(VWX\\), we need to determine the lengths of the opposite and adjacent sides to angle \\(V\\).\n\nFirst, let's identify the sides of the triangle relative to angle \\(V\\):\n- The hypotenuse is \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\).\n- One leg (adjacent to \\(V\\)) is \\(VW = 3\\).\n- The other leg (opposite to \\(V\\)) is \\(WX\\).\n\nWe need to find the length of \\(WX\\). We can use the Pythagorean theorem, which states:\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\nSolving for \\(WX^2\\):\n\\[\nWX^2 = 13 - 9\n\\]\n\\[\nWX^2 = 4\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nWX = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we have the lengths of the sides:\n- \\(VW = 3\\) (adjacent to \\(V\\))\n- \\(WX = 2\\) (opposite to \\(V\\))\n- \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\) (hypotenuse)\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\tan V\\):\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
